\begin_layout Standard
Engineers get taught a version of Fourier series in which 
\begin_inset Formula $a_{n}:=\frac{1}{L}\int_{-L}^{L}f(x)\cos\frac{n\pi x}{L}dx$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $b_{n}:=\frac{1}{L}\int_{-L}^{L}f(x)\sin\frac{n\pi x}{L}dx$
\end_inset

,
 and then we can express a periodic function as 
\begin_inset Formula $f=\frac{a_{0}}{2}+\sum_{n=1}^{\infty}\left(a_{n}\cos\frac{n\pi x}{L}+b_{n}\sin\frac{n\pi x}{L}\right)$
\end_inset

.
 We are spiritually superior to engineers,
 so we don't bother with this sine and cosine nonsense,
 which are shadows concealing the true nature of the divine.
 Rather,
 given 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

,
 we define (assuming period 
\begin_inset Formula $2\pi$
\end_inset

 for now) 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{f}(k):=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(\theta)\exp(-ik\theta)\:d\theta$
\end_inset

,
 and then our series is 
\begin_inset Formula $f(x)=\sum_{k=-\infty}^{\infty}\fhat(k)\exp(ik\theta)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
If a function were uniquely pinned down by its Fourier coëfficients,
 then we could say:
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

 have the same coëfficients,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $g$
\end_inset

 are equal.
 Then consider the difference 
\begin_inset Formula $h:=f-g$
\end_inset

.
 Putting both antecedent and consequent in terms of 
\begin_inset Formula $h$
\end_inset

:
 if 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall k\ \hat{h}(k)=0$
\end_inset

,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $h=0$
\end_inset

.
 That's not quite true,
 because a function can differ at finitely many points and still have the same integral and therefore the same Fourier coëfficients,
 but it's spiritually true:
 
\begin_inset Formula $f(\theta_{0})=0$
\end_inset

 when 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is continuous at 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta_{0}$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We should do some exercises.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
Ch1#2
\series default
.
 
\series bold
a
\series default
.
 
\emph on
Proposition
\emph default
.
 
\begin_inset Formula $f(\theta)\sim\fhat(0)+\sum_{k\ge1}\left(\fhat(k)+\fhat(-k)\right)\cos n\theta+i\left(\fhat(k)-\fhat(-k)\right)\sin n\theta$
\end_inset

,
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
Proof
\emph default
.
 This is intuitive enough that writing out the algebra shouldn't be difficult at all.
 We have 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{k=-\infty}^{\infty}\fhat(k)\exp(ik\theta)=\sum_{k=-\infty}^{\infty}\fhat(k)\left(\cos(k\theta)+i\sin(k\theta)\right)=\sum_{k=-\infty}^{\infty}\fhat(k)\cos(k\theta)+i\fhat(k)\sin(k\theta)$
\end_inset

.
 Then we'll separate the negative,
 positive,
 and zero parts of the integer argument.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
So we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\fhat(0)\left(\cancelto{1}{\cos(0)}+\cancel{\sin(0)}\right)+\sum_{k\ge1}\fhat(-k)\cos(-k\theta)+i\fhat(-k)\sin(-k\theta)+\sum_{k\ge1}\fhat(k)\cos(k\theta)+i\fhat(k)\sin(k\theta)$
\end_inset

 and then cosine is even and sine is odd,
 so that becomes 
\begin_inset Formula $\fhat(0)+\sum_{k\ge1}\fhat(-k)\cos(k\theta)-i\fhat(-k)\sin(k\theta)+\sum_{k\ge1}\fhat(k)\cos(k\theta)+i\fhat(k)\sin(k\theta)$
\end_inset

 which is 
\emph on
quod erat demonstrandum
\emph default
 after regrouping.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\checkmark$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
b
\series default
.
 
\emph on
Proposition
\emph default
.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is even,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $\fhat(k)=\fhat(-k)$
\end_inset

 and we have a cosine series.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
Proof
\emph default
.
 Suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is even.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{f}(k):=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(\theta)\exp(-ik\theta)\:d\theta$
\end_inset

.
 Can we integrate by parts (
\begin_inset Formula $\int u\,dv=uv-\int v\,du$
\end_inset

) even if we don't non-symbolically know the derivative of 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

?
 Presumably it doesn't matter:
 the point is that it should be the same if we swap 
\begin_inset Formula $k\leftrightarrow-k$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Assigning 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}\underbrace{f(\theta)}_{u:=}\underbrace{\exp(-ik\theta)}_{dv:=}\:d\theta$
\end_inset

 yields 
\begin_inset Formula $du:=f'(\theta)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $v:=\frac{-1}{ik}\exp(-ik\theta)$
\end_inset

,
 so the parts formula (setting the factor of 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{2\pi}$
\end_inset

 aside for a moment) yields 
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left.\underbrace{f(\theta)}_{u}\cdot\underbrace{\frac{-1}{ik}\exp(-ik\theta)}_{v}\right|_{\theta=-\pi}^{\pi}-\frac{-1}{ik}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}\underbrace{\exp(-ik\theta)}_{v}\cdot\underbrace{f'(\theta)}_{du}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
=\frac{-1}{ik}\cdot\left(f(\pi)\exp(-ik\pi)-\underbrace{f(-\pi)}_{=f(\pi)\mathrm{\,(b/c\,even)}}\exp(ik\pi)\right)-\frac{-1}{ik}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f'(\theta)\exp(-ik\theta)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{-1}{ik}\cdot\left(f(\pi)\exp(-ik\pi)-f(\pi)\exp(ik\pi)\right)-\frac{-1}{ik}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f'(\theta)\exp(-ik\theta)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\frac{-f(\pi)}{ik}\cdot\left(\exp(-ik\pi)-\exp(ik\pi)\right)-\frac{-1}{ik}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f'(\theta)\exp(-ik\theta)
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
But 
\begin_inset Formula $\exp(-ik\pi)-\exp(ik\pi)$
\end_inset

 should just be zero,
 so the boundary term vanishes.
 (Whether 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 is even or odd only determines whether it works out as 
\begin_inset Formula $-1-(-1)=-1+1=0$
\end_inset

 or 
\begin_inset Formula $1-1=0$
\end_inset

.) We're left with 
\begin_inset Formula $-\frac{-1}{ik}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f'(\theta)\exp(-ik\theta)$
\end_inset

,
 which we should be able to integrate by parts 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

the other way
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $-\frac{-1}{ik}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}\underbrace{\exp(-ik\theta)}_{u}\underbrace{f'(\theta)}_{dv}$
\end_inset

 yielding 
\begin_inset Formula $du:=-ik\exp(-ik\theta)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $v:=f(\theta)$
\end_inset

.
 Then
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left.\underbrace{\exp(-ik\theta)f(\theta)}_{uv}\right|_{\theta:=-\pi}^{\pi}+\underbrace{ik\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(\theta)\exp(-ik\theta)}_{\int v\,du}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Except—
aren't we just going in circles here?
 (The boundary term is going to be zero again,
 and 
\begin_inset Formula $\cancel{-}\frac{\cancel{-}1}{\cancel{ik}}\cancel{ik}=1$
\end_inset

.) Doing a long calculation to show that 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(\theta)\exp(-ik\theta)\:d\theta$
\end_inset

 equals itself doesn't help us at all!
 (Although it is perhaps reässuring that I'm not writing total nonsense?)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
What would be non-circular would be to do the calculation for 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{f}(-k)$
\end_inset

 (
\emph on
not
\emph default
 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{f}(k)$
\end_inset

).
 So 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}\underbrace{f(\theta)}_{u:=}\underbrace{\exp(ik\theta)}_{dv:=}\:d\theta$
\end_inset

 yields 
\begin_inset Formula $du:=f'(\theta)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $v:=\frac{1}{ik}\exp(ik\theta)$
\end_inset

,
 so we have
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
\begin_inset Formula 
\[
\left.\underbrace{f(\theta)}_{u}\cdot\underbrace{\frac{1}{ik}\exp(ik\theta)}_{v}\right|_{\theta=-\pi}^{\pi}-\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}\underbrace{\frac{1}{ik}\exp(ik\theta)}_{v}\cdot\underbrace{f'(\theta)}_{du}
\]

\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The boundary term is 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{ik}\left(f(\pi)\exp(ik\pi)-f(-\pi)\exp(ik\pi)\right)=\frac{1}{ik}f(\pi)\left(\exp(ik\pi)-\exp(ik\pi)\right)=\frac{1}{ik}f(\pi)\cdot0=0$
\end_inset

 again.
 So now I guess we're doing parts 
\emph on
again
\emph default
,
 on 
\begin_inset Formula $-\frac{1}{ik}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}\underbrace{\exp(ik\theta)}_{u}\cdot\underbrace{f'(\theta)}_{dv}$
\end_inset

,
 yielding 
\begin_inset Formula $du:=ik\exp(ik\theta)$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $v:=f(\theta)$
\end_inset

,
 to get 
\begin_inset Formula $\left.\underbrace{\exp(ik\theta)}_{u}\cdot\underbrace{f(\theta)}_{v}\right|_{\theta:=-\pi}^{\pi}-\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}\underbrace{f(\theta)}_{v}\underbrace{ik\exp(ik\theta)}_{du}$
\end_inset

.
 That same boundary term is going to vanish again,
 and bringing back those constant factors we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{2\pi}\cdot\cancel{-}\frac{1}{\cancel{ik}}\cdot\cancel{-}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(\theta)\cancel{ik}\exp(ik\theta)=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(\theta)\exp(ik\theta)$
\end_inset

 ...
 but that's just going in a circle again!
 We want 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(\theta)\exp(-ik\theta)$
\end_inset

,
 with the negative sign inside the exponential.
 In retrospect,
 it would seem that 
\begin_inset Quotes eld
\end_inset

integrate by parts,
 then integrate by parts again `the other way'
\begin_inset Quotes erd
\end_inset

 was not going to be helpful no matter where we start from;
 there has to be some other trick.
 But what?
 The solution somehow needs to depend on 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 being even—
and we did use that fact in determining that the boundary term vanishes.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Claude Opus 5 says that we should actually be doing a change-of-variables like 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi:=-\theta$
\end_inset

 here (the changes in the limits should cancel with 
\begin_inset Formula $d\varphi=-d\theta$
\end_inset

 and only the integrand gets negated,
 which gets absorbed by evenness),
 and that,
 more generally,
 when the hypothesis is a symmetry statement,
 that's the transformation to apply.
 Also,
 we're not even allowed to use parts like that because we don't know that 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is differentiable.
 
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Let's do that.
 We have 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{f}(k):=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(\theta)\exp(-ik\theta)\:d\theta$
\end_inset

,
 and we substitute 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi:=-\theta$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $d\varphi=-d\theta$
\end_inset

 to get 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{f}(k)=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{\pi}^{-\pi}-f(-\varphi)\exp(ik\varphi)\,d\varphi=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(\varphi)\exp(ik\varphi)\,d\varphi$
\end_inset

,
 so 
\begin_inset Formula $\fhat(-k)=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(\varphi)\exp(-ik\varphi)\,d\varphi=\fhat(k)$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\checkmark$
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
The remaining question is,
 why is that a cosine series?
 That comes from part a.
 We have as a proposition that 
\begin_inset Formula $f(\theta)\sim\fhat(0)+\sum_{k\ge1}\left(\fhat(k)+\fhat(-k)\right)\cos n\theta+i\left(\fhat(k)-\fhat(-k)\right)\sin n\theta$
\end_inset

.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $\fhat(k)=\fhat(-k)$
\end_inset

,
 that becomes 
\begin_inset Formula $\fhat(0)+\sum_{k\ge1}2\fhat(k)\cos n\theta+i\cancel{\left(\fhat(k)-\fhat(k)\right)}\sin n\theta$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\checkmark$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
c
\series default
.
 
\emph on
Proposition
\emph default
.
 If 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is odd,
 then 
\begin_inset Formula $\fhat(k)=-\fhat(-k)$
\end_inset

 and we have a sine series.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
Proof
\emph default
.
 Now that we've been straightened out from being retarded,
 it's presumably the same deal.
 The 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi=-\theta$
\end_inset

 substitution again brings us to 
\begin_inset Formula $\hat{f}(k)=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{\pi}^{-\pi}-f(-\varphi)\exp(ik\varphi)\,d\varphi$
\end_inset

,
 but this time 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is odd,
 so we get 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}-f(\varphi)\exp(ik\varphi)\,d\varphi$
\end_inset

,
 so 
\begin_inset Formula $\fhat(-k)=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}-f(\varphi)\exp(-ik\varphi)\,d\varphi=-\fhat(k)$
\end_inset

,
 and thus 
\begin_inset Formula $\fhat(k)=-\fhat(-k)$
\end_inset

,
 which is a sine series because 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{k\ge1}\left(\fhat(k)+\fhat(-k)\right)\cos n\theta+i\left(\fhat(k)-\fhat(-k)\right)\sin n\theta$
\end_inset

 becomes 
\begin_inset Formula $\sum_{k\ge1}\cancel{\left(\fhat(k)-\fhat(k)\right)}\cos n\theta+2i\fhat(k)\sin n\theta$
\end_inset

.
 
\begin_inset Formula $\checkmark$
\end_inset


\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
d
\series default
.
 
\emph on
Proposition
\emph default
.
 Suppose that for 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta\in\R$
\end_inset

,
 
\begin_inset Formula $f(\theta+\pi)=f(\theta)$
\end_inset

.
 Then 
\begin_inset Formula $\fhat(k)=0$
\end_inset

 for odd 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
Proof
\emph default
.
 We were advised that when the hypothesis is a symmetry statement,
 that's the transformation to apply,
 so for lack of any better ideas,
 we'll try substituting 
\begin_inset Formula $\varphi:=\theta+\pi$
\end_inset

,
 which implies 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta=\varphi-\pi$
\end_inset

.
 Then 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(\theta)\exp(-ik\theta)\:d\theta$
\end_inset

 becomes 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(\varphi-\pi)\exp(-ik(\varphi-\pi))\:d\varphi=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(\varphi-\pi)\exp((k\pi-k\varphi)i)\:d\varphi$
\end_inset

.
 Then for odd 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

,
 we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(\varphi)\cancelto{-1}{\exp(k\pi i)}\cdot\left(-\exp(k\varphi i)\right)\:d\varphi=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(\varphi)\exp(k\varphi i)\:d\varphi$
\end_inset

.
 It seems like all that did was get rid of the negative sign inside of the exponential,
 which does not obviously help us.
 Maybe the transformation was the wrong tack,
 and the point is that the periodicity will make the antiderivative at 
\begin_inset Formula $\pi$
\end_inset

 and 
\begin_inset Formula $-\pi$
\end_inset

 come out the same,
 so the integral is zero?
 But I don't know how to do the integral:
 we can't use parts with 
\begin_inset Formula $u:=f(\theta)$
\end_inset

 if we don't know that 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is differentiable (per Claude's earlier warning) and parts with 
\begin_inset Formula $dv:=f(\theta)$
\end_inset

 doesn't help us if the periodicity constraint doesn't necessarily apply to the antiderivative 
\begin_inset Formula $F$
\end_inset

.
 Halp?
\begin_inset Newline newline
\end_inset


\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\series bold
e
\series default
.
 
\emph on
Proposition
\emph default
.
 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is real-valued if and only if 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall k\ \overline{\fhat(k)}=\fhat(-k)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard

\emph on
Proof
\emph default
.
 (
\begin_inset Formula $\Rightarrow$
\end_inset

) Suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is real-valued.
 Then 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{\fhat(k)}=\overline{\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(\theta)\exp(-ik\theta)\:d\theta}=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}\overline{f(\theta)}\overline{\exp(-ik\theta)}\:d\theta=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(\theta)\exp(ik\theta)\:d\theta=\fhat(-k)$
\end_inset

.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
(
\begin_inset Formula $\Leftarrow$
\end_inset

) Suppose 
\begin_inset Formula $\forall k\ \overline{\fhat(k)}=\fhat(-k)$
\end_inset

.
 That is to say,
 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(\theta)\exp(-ik\theta)\:d\theta}=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(\theta)\exp(ik\theta)\:d\theta$
\end_inset

.
 We can pull the overline into the integral like 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}\overline{f(\theta)}\overline{\exp(-ik\theta)}\:d\theta$
\end_inset

,
 and into the exponential like 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{\exp(-ik\theta)}=\exp(ik\theta)$
\end_inset

.
 (We know that 
\begin_inset Formula $\theta$
\end_inset

 is real,
 so 
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{\theta}=\theta$
\end_inset

.) So we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}\overline{f(\theta)}\exp(ik\theta)\:d\theta=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(\theta)\exp(ik\theta)\:d\theta$
\end_inset

.
 It's tempting to just declare that this implies
\begin_inset Formula $\overline{f(\theta)}=f(\theta)$
\end_inset

 and therefore that 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is real-valued,
 but I'm worried that that's a sloppy inference even if it has to be spiritually true.
 For example,
 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 being non-real at a single point wouldn't affect the value of the integral.
 I'm daydreaming that there should be an elegant proof that if 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is continuous,
 then it can only be non-real-valued on an interval,
 not a single point,
 and then there would have to be some 
\begin_inset Formula $k$
\end_inset

 that exposes it?
 But we don't even know that 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is continuous,
 only Riemann-integrable.
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
Good sign that I'm not retarded:
 Prof.
 Lai actually addressed this concern in class on Monday 31 August!
 He said that we could assume that 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is continuous (or if it's not continuous,
 then it's real-valued merely almost everywhere).
 (Is the implication that the venerable Stein and Shakarchi made a mistake in an exercise this simple?)
\end_layout

\begin_layout Standard
We're probably not quite done with the proof.
 Suppose that 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is continuous,
 and we have 
\begin_inset Formula $\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}\overline{f(\theta)}\exp(ik\theta)\:d\theta=\frac{1}{2\pi}\int_{-\pi}^{\pi}f(\theta)\exp(ik\theta)\:d\theta$
\end_inset

.
 Suppose for a contradiction that 
\begin_inset Formula $f$
\end_inset

 is not real-valued.
